As shown in Figure 4B, log unit 408A receives the first attenuation value ηi, providing the value ln(η to the multiplier 400C. Multiplier 400C also receives the value of c and transmits the associated product 

 ) to the adder 404B. The second attenuation value η2, which supplies the input value to log unit 408B, undergoes similar processing. Log unit 408B provides the converted attenuation value ln(η2) to multiplier 400D, which provides the product of (l -c)\n r]2e"Pl ) to the adder 404B. Adder 404B calculates the sum cln(^,e'Pl )+ (l - c)ln(^2e'P2 ), which is processed through the exponential unit 412 to become the apparent attenuation value ηc. The value ηc is then converted to an attenuation resistivity value according to known techniques, thus providing a new resistivity value associated with the depth of investigation Dc. 

 As one of ordinary skill in the art would recognize, the log units 408A-B and exponential unit 412 may be eliminated in the flow diagram of Figure 4B if desired. Accordingly, the adder 404B may be replace with a multiplier, and the multipliers 400C-D may be replaced with exponential units which utilize the input values as the base values and either c or 1-c as the exponents. Figure 5 illustrates an alternative embodiment, under which the processor 204 calculates the apparent attenuation value ηc and phase value φc defined in equations (4) and (5), using the complex received signals ηλe" and η2e'ψl as input values. Although the flow diagrams of Figures 4 A-B may be used interchangeably with the operations shown in Figure 5, the present embodiment may be particularly useful if the composite attenuation values ηl5 η2 and/or composite phase values φ1; φ are not readily available. The flow diagram comprises a pair of log units 508A-B, a pair of multipliers 500A-B, an adder 504, an exponential unit 512, an attenuation detector 516, and a phase detector 520.
